@echo off
REM GENERATED by scripts/detached_launch.py -- edit the generator, not this file.
REM Score the shipped-expression-heads arm against its FROZEN bar.
REM Launched 2026-11-03 for the unattended window. Projected 4.64 h.
REM 
REM WHAT THIS RUNS, AND WHY IT COSTS WHAT IT DOES. Five shipped PromoterAtlas
REM prediction heads, applied ZERO-SHOT to 44,106 GSE144621 peak tiles, on the
REM FAITHFUL per-window route: each head is geometry-locked to 86 positions against
REM a 150-position substrate, so every tile is scored over 150-86+1 = 65 windows.
REM The cheap route (one full latent, then slice) is 19.3x faster and was REJECTED
REM by the frozen bar, because the margin-derived rank tolerance 0.999 is missed by
REM tslpred-kosuri2013 at 0.9888 -- the tolerance was written down before being
REM pointed at the probe. The arm pays 4.64 h instead of 0.24 h.
REM 
REM BOTH ROOTS ARE ON C:, AND THE SECOND ONE WAS A MEASURED CORRECTION. The first
REM launch of this job FAILED in under a second with rc=1: the default MPRA_CACHE is
REM D:/dna_decode_cache/mpra and the D: volume is NOT attached (disk 1 is an empty
REM card reader, 0 GB, No Media). The plan recorded D: as attached with 823.8 GB
REM free, which was true when it was written and is not now -- attachment on this
REM host is per-session and never inherited. The substrate was already present on C:
REM at data/mpra, which is the same location run_tile_conv_detached.bat uses.
REM 
REM THE LOCAL COPY WAS VERIFIED BY CONTENT, NOT BY NAME. A directory of the right
REM name is not evidence of the right contents, and external_root refuses to
REM auto-substitute a root for exactly that reason. load_arm_cohort re-derives the
REM cohort and REFUSES any count other than 44,106, because the embedding cache is
REM indexed positionally and an off-by-one cohort would pair every feature row with
REM the wrong embedding row and still score. It returned 44,106 tiles at 150 bp over
REM 2,433 peaks from the C: copy, so the substitution is checked rather than assumed.
REM 
REM PA_REPO POINTS AT THE DURABLE MIRROR, NOT THE TEMP CLONE. The original clone
REM lives under AppData/Local/Temp/pa_probe, which a reboot can remove mid-run.
REM Step 1 mirrored the weights to data/pa_clone_durable on C: and verified them by
REM hash and by loading. Both PA_REPO and MPRA_CACHE are read at module import time,
REM so they are set here rather than passed as arguments.
REM 
REM ON C:, DELIBERATELY INVERTING THE D: RULE. This host has six recorded long-run
REM deaths; four were STATUS_IN_PAGE_ERROR with torch mmapping weights off a USB
REM volume, and the one ~12 h survivor kept its data on C:. The mirror is 87.7 MB
REM and the MPRA cache 96 MB against 38.7 GB free, so the D: rule's purpose --
REM keeping bulk caches off a chronically full disk -- is intact.
REM 
REM RESUMABLE, AND THAT IS LOAD-BEARING. The extraction streams: per batch of 64 it
REM computes the latent once, applies all five heads over all 65 windows, reduces,
REM and discards the latent, because the full-cohort latent would be 3.39 GB. The
REM sidecar is rewritten after every batch and a heartbeat after each, so an
REM unexplained death costs one batch and a relaunch resumes. A capped run stamps
REM the block PARTIAL and the scorer REFUSES to read it as the arm's result.
REM 
REM READ THE STATE WITH: python scripts/detached_launch.py status expression_heads
REM
REM Launch DETACHED from PowerShell so it outlives the session:
REM   Start-Process -FilePath <repo>\run_expression_heads_detached.bat -WorkingDirectory <repo> -WindowStyle Hidden

setlocal
cd /d "%~dp0"

set "LOG=%~dp0data\unattended\expression_heads.log"
set "SENTINEL=%~dp0data\unattended\expression_heads.sentinel"
set "LOCK=%~dp0data\unattended\expression_heads.lock"

REM A sentinel from a PREVIOUS run must go before this one starts: a stale EXIT=0 left
REM beside a live job reads as COMPLETE and would hide the run entirely.
if exist "%SENTINEL%" del "%SENTINEL%"

REM Unbuffered, or a death loses the last minutes of the log -- which is precisely the
REM window that explains the death.
set PYTHONUNBUFFERED=1

REM Job environment, set inside setlocal so it cannot leak to the caller.
REM PA_REPO and friends are read at module import, so a runtime argument
REM could not reach them.
set "MPRA_CACHE=C:\Users\Laptop Portable\pythonprojects\dna_decode\data\mpra"
set "PA_REPO=C:\Users\Laptop Portable\pythonprojects\dna_decode\data\pa_clone_durable"

echo ==== start %DATE% %TIME% ==== > "%LOG%"

REM Guarded by the PID-bearing run lock: a second launch over a long window exits 9
REM rather than running two concurrent torch jobs, which is this host's documented
REM access-violation hazard. `run` (not `acquire`) holds the lock for the CHILD's
REM lifetime -- an `acquire` process exits at once and its pid is reclaimed as stale.
uv run python scripts/glm_run_lock.py run "%LOCK%" --label "expression_heads" -- uv run python -m scripts.expression_heads_score >> "%LOG%" 2>&1

set "RC=%ERRORLEVEL%"

echo ==== end %DATE% %TIME% rc=%RC% ==== >> "%LOG%"

REM THE REDIRECT IS WRITTEN BEFORE THE ECHO ON PURPOSE. `echo EXIT=%RC%>>file` expands
REM to `echo EXIT=0>>file`, and cmd reads a digit immediately preceding `>>` as a FILE
REM HANDLE -- it redirects handle 0 (stdin) and leaves the sentinel EMPTY. Measured on
REM run_glm_anchor_detached.bat, not theorised.
>>"%SENTINEL%" echo EXIT=%RC%

endlocal
exit /b %RC%
